 1.1 On one closed curvilinear triangle $\Delta$ of the subdivision, pull the connection back by the smooth restriction of $H$ and write its two coefficient matrices as $A_s=\omega(H_s)$ and $A_t=\omega(H_t)$. For a smooth family of paths inside $\Delta$, let $U_t=-A_tU$, $U(s,0)=I$, and put $K=U_s+A_sU$. Smooth dependence in [F4] permits this differentiation, and [F5] gives uniqueness for the linear equation satisfied by $K$. The product rule gives $K_t+A_tK=-(\partial_sA_t-\partial_tA_s+[A_s,A_t])U=-H^*F(\partial_s,\partial_t)U$. Here [F3] identifies the pulled-back two-form as the curvature of the supplier connection. [given, F2, F3, F4, F5, algebra]
 
-1.2 Since $\operatorname{rank}(dH)\le1$ on $\Delta$, $H_s,H_t$ are linearly dependent and the alternating two-form satisfies $H^*F(\partial_s,\partial_t)=F(H_s,H_t)=0$. Fix a vertex $q_0$ and join it to each $q\in\Delta$ by the image of the straight radial path in the standard-simplex parametrization. The associated transports $Q(q)$ exist globally by [F5] and depend smoothly on $q$: apply [F4] locally in the endpoint parameter and continue over finitely many subintervals of the compact path interval, using uniqueness on overlaps. For each radial path, [F3] and the ODE give $(Q^{\mathsf T}\eta Q)'=0$, hence $Q(q)^{\mathsf T}\eta Q(q)=\eta$ and $Q(q)$ is invertible. Step 1.1 on this radial family with fixed start gives $dQ=-H^*\omega\,Q$. Thus transport from $q_1$ to $q_2$ in $\Delta$ is $Q(q_2)Q(q_1)^{-1}$ by [F5], and the triangle boundary transport is identity. [given, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra]
