-2.1 The finite curvilinear-triangle subdivision is a finite connected CW complex $K$; choose the lower-left corner as basepoint and a maximal tree in its one-skeleton. The outer boundary loop contracts to that basepoint by the straight-line contraction of the parameter square, so [F6] makes its fundamental-group class the identity. By [F7], $\pi_1(K)$ has generators given by non-tree edge loops closed along the tree and relators given by based triangular face boundaries. For each free generator $x_e$, assign the inverse transport of its based edge loop and extend to a homomorphism $\Phi$ from the free group; [F2]'s path-composition law gives $\Phi(w)=U_w^{-1}$ for every edge word $w$. These transports are invertible because along each segment [F3] and the ODE give $(U^{\mathsf T}\eta U)'=-U^{\mathsf T}(\omega^{\mathsf T}\eta+\eta\omega)U=0$, hence $U^{\mathsf T}\eta U=\eta$; reversing the ODE gives $U_{\bar\alpha}=U_\alpha^{-1}$. A based face relator is a tree path, a triangular boundary and the reverse tree path, so step 1.2 makes its transport and its $\Phi$-image the identity. The boundary word is identity in the presentation by [F6]–[F8], and [F9] writes it as a finite product of conjugates of face relators and their inverses; all of these map to $I$, so $U_\lambda=I$. The side edges of the square are constant by fixed endpoints, giving $U_\lambda=U_{\gamma_1}^{-1}U_{\gamma_0}$ and $U_{\gamma_0}(1)=U_{\gamma_1}(1)$. [given, F1, F2, F3, F6, F7, F8, F9, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
